Determine which lifecycle and ordering responsibilities are currently hidden inside runtime snapshot construction/application and submission completion. Trace apply_runtime_state_snapshot and its overview/presentation-transition wrappers, RuntimeActiveRunController and RuntimeSubmitController, worker result delivery, Session replacement/detached states and TUI shutdown. Cover new/clear/resume/switch/fork/branch operations, reloads that change state, queued submissions and delayed completions. Explain the identity and authority of each worker/result/event before and after a session transition and precisely where stale work is rejected or could still publish.

Inventory all transition side effects: grants and prompt authority, attachments, draft/stash state, transcript replacement, search/selection/scroll anchors, overview, selector state, subagent views, plugin/prompt cancellation, renderer invalidation and outstanding callbacks. For each, establish whether observing only the latest canonical value is sufficient or every transition must be processed exactly once in order. Propose an explicit identity/revision and transition-control contract compatible with UnlockedData without moving backend authority into presentation caches. Identify required lock ordering, lifetime barriers and worker cancellation/join responsibilities, especially notification safety during frontend destruction.

Trace the two /exit statistics publications from snapshot application and completion refresh, distinguishing required final usage/persistence from redundant presentation work. Produce a lifecycle/ordering artifact, registered in contents, with concrete call chains, transition scenarios, proposed preservation rules and deterministic test seams. Cover rapid transitions, old-session completion, failed transitions, cancellation during interaction and shutdown. Record uncertain behavior as targeted investigation or test requirements, not guesses. This goal produces design evidence only; do not move quit checks, remove cleanup or change runtime behavior while auditing.
